svek <svek@gmx.net> wrote:
> "This DEC Alpha board requires ( 4 ) 72 pin 36 bit sims.
> There is eight slots. You must install ( 4 ) at a time.
> 16MB to 512MB memory array--One bank of either 4 (128-bit) or 8 (256-bit)
> commodity, 36-bit, 70-ns SIMMs"

   you need 72 pin fpm-ram with true parity.
   you can either put in 4 identical simms oder 8. if you put in just four,
the memory-bus is 128 bit wide, if you put in eight, it will be 256 bit
wide. note that you have to put in eight identical simms (same size and
architecture.) there is no possibility to put in less than four or something
like 6 simms.

> It does say that there are to two banks of each for slots, one bank has to
> be full of equal RAMs in order to function (i.e. 4 slots)

   that's true. there is a jumper on the mainboard, as far as i can remeber.
if it is set (and you have eight simms), they need to be identical, and the
bus will be 256 bit wide. if it is not set, you need four identical simms in
the first bank and, again, four identical simms in the second bank. in this
case, the bus will only be 128 bit wide.

> each slot can handle 16MB to 512MB of memory (anyone who knows exactly how
> much memory this card can handle as a total?

   i think each _bank_ can handle from 16 to 512 mb. i think the total
ram-size is limited to 1 gb.

> If someone could point me into a manufactor or even a specification
> direction of these memories it would be great.

   kingston. they have exactly what you need at horrendous prices ...
   you'd be better off to search through ebay for fpm-ram with true parity.


