> Yes, you can put a 32-bit PCI card into a 64-bit slot.  (As I
> type this, my UP2000 has 32-bit PCI cards (graphics and BTTV) in
> the 64-bit slows.)  I checked out the PCI specification before
> doing it.  IIRC, there is a pin in the 32-bit side of the
> connector that the card drives to tell the mobo whether there the
> card is 32-bit or 64-bit.  As long as the mobo chipset follows
> the specification correctly, everything just works.
> 
> Don't quote me on this, and don't try it without studying the
> specification yourself, but I seem to recall that you could also
> do the reverse, a 64-bit card in a 32-bit slot.

You can indeed do that - I've done it myself, at least on Compact PCI 
systems - providing there is nothing on the motherboard preventing the card 
from seating itself correctly - for example tall components directly under 
the additional 64 bit connections.

What you do need to be careful of is putting 33 MHz PCI cards on a 66 MHz 
bus segment - they'll work, but any 66 MHz cards on the same bus segment 
will degrade to 33 MHz operation, as will any cards on downstream 66 MHz bus 
segments. Depending on the PCI bridge chip used, fitting a 33 MHz card 
shouldn't cause any loss of performance on upstream bus segments.



