Per Jessen wrote in message <3C22733A.7080305@computer.org>...
>Per Jessen wrote:
>
>> Hi,
>>
>> Can anyone help with some pointers on where to get some info ?
>> The board is Digital, has 5 EISA slots, and a 21064-AA CPU.
>
>
>Apparently this system a socalled Jensen ? The only Alpha system built
>with EISA slots ?
>I've found quite a bit of material on it, but I'm still left with
>the questions regarding the power-supply:
>


A number of earlier Alphaservers were made with EISA slots, usually with
one or two PCI slots also.  The -A updates reversed and had more PCI than
EISA (AlphaServer 1000 vs. 1000a, etc).  The DEC 2000-300, aka Jensen,
aka PC-AXP/150 or somesuch precedes the Alphaserver line, and was the
only EISA/ISA only system I can recall.  It is an antique, without overly
good
software or hardware support.

This system requires true parity memory, 60-70ns, with four SIMMs in each of
two banks.  Max supported SIMM size I remember from years ago when we had
one was 16MB (4x36) so max memory is 128MB.

>> Also, next thing I'll be doing is to look for a power-supply -
>> the power-connectors look similar to those of an AT-style motherboard,
>> but could I use a regular AT powersupply with an Alpha board ?
>


Most Alpha motherboards require a 3.3V power supply in addition to the
normal
+/- 5 and +/- 12V.  There were specially made supplies for some Micron
servers
and DEC PC64 (and perhaps other?)  MLBs that had two such additional
connectors.
If you check the connections, its probably 3 contiguous pins providing 3.3v,
the other
three contiguous pins providing ground, but I cannot verify that for a
Jensen.

These supplies that do AT connectors with 3.3v are usually very pricy, but
there was
an IBM power supply from their PPC 604 evaluation boards, 200 watt, soft
power
switch (which you have to jury-rig) being sold by www.pcsurplusonline.com
cheap.
They sometimes sell through Ebay.  I bought one from them, it works fine
with a PC64,
but I have no other connection to the company.  I do NOT think it is
equivalent to the
power supply that came in the original DEC system; I believe that was a 300
watt unit,
so who knows if the 200 will suffice...

This is truly an old crufty board.  I only recall support for Adaptex 1742
SCSI and some
crufty Compaq Qvision video cards, and I think the DEC Etherworks/EISA
10Base-T
card.  You should seriously consider investing in a better/newer
one, because the cost of getting a bare Jensen MLB running will be
startling.  These
systems, _complete_ in case, with power supply, NIC, SCSI, and video, have
gone
for under $125 on Ebay, maybe even less.  A bare motherboard... its going to
be a
real chore.

>
>This board has the usual 12pin connector, that normally takes 2 6pin
>plugs. But it also has a 2nd 6pin connector, so 18pin total.
>
>Does anyone know where to get hold of a manual for this board ?
>


No, sorry.  You can get some info, perhaps still get the EISA configuration
utility
floppies from somewhere on the Compaq site, but I have no bookmarks for it,
and don't
have time to dig.  Good luck...

>
>MTIA,
>Per Jessen, Zurich.
>




